{"id":"h147","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A sphere, a cube, and a cylinder, all of equal mass, are released from rest from the top of a short incline. The surface of the incline is extremely slick, so much so that the objects do not rotate when released, but rather slide with negligible friction. Which reaches the base of the incline first?\nIs the correct answer \"The object in the middle\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer to whether it is 'the object in the middle' is NO.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"None of the three objects is uniquely in the middle.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"All three objects reach the base at the same time.","confidence":0.96,"lean":-1,"theta":1.508,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"In rolling problems, shape matters because it sets the rotational inertia.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Someone might expect the cylinder or sphere to rank in some order, with one landing in the middle.","confidence":0.15,"lean":1,"theta":0.2356,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Because the objects do not rotate, no energy goes into rotation and each acts like a point mass sliding down a frictionless ramp.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The only force along the incline is mg sinθ, so every object has acceleration g sinθ independent of mass and shape.","confidence":0.96,"lean":-1,"theta":1.508,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Starting from rest over the same distance with the same acceleration, they arrive simultaneously.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The no-rotation, frictionless condition is the deciding consideration, leaving no basis for ordering.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j